  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nIn the wake of this afternoon's pre-frontal trough passage,\nexpect light showers and gusty wind with a tight pressure \ngradient and mixing. Wind gusts should hold in the 25-35 mph\nrange in the lowlands and possibly up to 40 mph in the\nridges. The probability for advisory criteria >46 mph remains \nlow.\n\nA deepening upper-level trough is forecast to track across the \nUpper Ohio Valley region tonight into tomorrow morning. Overall\ncoverage of showers should decrease into the overnight hours,\nthough some scattered lake enhanced showers are possible within\nthe cold advection regime. The precipitation should be tapering\noff by the time the coldest air arrives Saturday morning. With\nthat said, there is a potential for a few snowflakes mixing in \nnorth of I-80 and in the ridges with temperatures dropping into\nthe upper 30s, but ground temperatures will be too warm for any\nflakes to stick.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe upper-level trough should exit the East Coast by late \nSaturday night/early Sunday morning, as the surface low \ndeepens/closes off the Carolina coast. Surface high pressure is \nexpected to build across the Upper Ohio Valley region, with \nlight wind and mainly clear skies. This should result in \nefficient radiational cooling Saturday night resulting in a \nfrost/freeze potential. A Freeze Watch has been issued for\nregion effective on Saturday night. Some frost will be possible\nagain Sunday night with surface high pressure overhead.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nModel ensembles indicate the upper-level closed low will break \naway from the trough and persist off of the SE Coast. Over the\nWestern CONUS, another upper-level trough will become \nrelatively stationary by Tuesday, following a Tropical Storm\nSimon. Areas from the Midwest to the Upper Ohio Valley region \nshould be under a ridge, with dry weather expected from Sunday \ninto late next week. Temperatures are also expected to moderate \nback to near or just above seasonable levels by mid week.",
